WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.060
Imagine planning your dream cruise by chatting with an ai that already knows every ship detail and can book it for you. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 187. Princess Cruise is parked in official trip planner inside ChatGPT, Travel Daily News reports. You type or talk in plain language instead of clicking through a maze of website menus. Picture a travel agent who sits in the same chat you already use for homework and random questions.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:34.880
You describe a family trip with kids or a quiet adult getaway, and it proposes ships,

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.320
dates, and activities.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.520
It can even complete pieces of the booking for you.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.180
The helper plugs into the crew's company's own systems, so the options are live and current,

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:46.500
not fuzzy guesses.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.040
That is like checking the actual store shelf instead of remembering last month's sales sign.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.460
Artificial Intelligence or AI is moving here from answering trivia to finishing a real

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:04.980
world task. For a teen mapping future jobs, that points toward AI-powered customer service

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:10.540
and travel tech. For anyone planning a trip, the comparison grind gets a lot shorter.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.880
Try it right now. Open chat GPT on your phone or computer and type,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.460
help me plan a seven-day Caribbean cruise for my family.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.220
The feature lives in the normal chat, so you do not install anything new.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:29.740
Voice calls work in that same window, and you can speak preferences while it pulls live

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.160
availability from Princess.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:37.840
The integration runs on a model called GPT-6 Astra, which is the specific language program

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:38.720
doing the predicting.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.460
Think of a language model as supercharged auto-complete.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:48.400
It guesses the next useful sentence, except it has practiced on so much language that it

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.220
can plan a trip.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:55.920
GPT-6 Astra runs on its own cloud computer, meaning a remote machine in a data center,

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.720
not the chip in your pocket. It also learns from user feedback over time,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:06.300
so later suggestions drift closer to what people actually choose. You can ask a general

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:11.220
question about a destination, then lock in a specific cabin without leaving the app.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.220
The whole flow feels like a conversation instead of a long form.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:20.460
text a classmate all afternoon and you stop restating the assignment because the

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:25.360
thread already holds it. An AI can keep a similar running memory across many

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.420
chats and even across different tools. It can hold that you like beach days, your

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.980
budget range, and that younger siblings come along, then use that the next time

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.900
travel comes up. That memory is stored as patterns the model learned not as a

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.480
video recording of your voice. Compare it to a teacher who notices you always pick

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:50.460
the same lunch table. They remember the pattern, not a film of every sandwich. When a new request

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:55.940
arrives, the AI checks those patterns fast, and aims for an answer that feels personal instead

00:02:55.940 --> 00:03:01.260
of generic. Training on huge amounts of text is how it recognizes common travel themes,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:07.500
and then applies them to your details. Over repeated chats, the model updates those patterns based

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.560
on what you liked or changed. Follow-ups work for the same reason. You can ask about the one with the

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.880
water slide without repeating the whole plan.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:21.220
The same memory trick is what lets AI agents, meaning programs that take a sequence of

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:25.140
steps toward a goal, work with people without dropping earlier steps.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:30.720
Once you see it as pattern matching with a long thread, the magic feeling settles into something

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:31.880
you can actually follow.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.620
Want a speed boost inside the same app?

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.900
Keyboard shortcuts act like secret fast forward buttons, so you barely touch the mouse.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.300
House. One combo starts a brand new chat instantly, so old threads do not hitch a ride.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.360
Another copies the last answer with a single press, which is handy when you are gathering

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:56.260
ideas for school. A third copies only the last code block if the reply includes programming,

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.700
so you skip the scroll through a giant message. On a Windows laptop, go to the chat GPT website,

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:08.400
then press Ctrl-Shift-O to start fresh. On a Mac, that is Command-Shift-O.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.620
Next, try Ctrl-Shift-C to copy the latest reply without hunting for it.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.580
A community post on Reddit ranks the rest.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:22.260
Ctrl-K searches past chats, and Ctrl-Shift-I opens custom instructions,

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:26.560
which are the notes you leave about how you want chat GPT to talk to you.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.320
Shift-Escape jumps the cursor back to the message box after you click an answer,

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:41.760
which saves extra mouse travel. The lowest ranked shortcut deletes the current chat and sits close to a common delete word command, so export anything important first.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.760
Test them one by one during your next homework session and notice how much less clicking you need.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:57.080
Some people are going further and walking chat GPT through their whole life story. They answer dozens of questions about childhood, friends, and goals.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:00.120
then get back patterns they never spotted themselves.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.280
The AI acts like a patient listener that does not get bored

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.760
and does not drop what you said 20 minutes ago.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.580
It can point out strengths you shrugged off

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.120
or habits that keep repeating.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:13.940
One Reddit user turns spoken answers

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.220
into a numbered list of 25 to 30 questions covering

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:21.140
every life stage, with each answer taking about 20 minutes.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.000
Open chat GPT, start a new chat and type,

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.480
Ask me 25 questions about my life so far, and then give me insights at the end.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:34.300
Answer honestly over a few sessions and watch what it notices.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:38.940
Those answers stay between you and the AI unless you choose to share them.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.880
That same user said the model returned both positive patterns and honest critiques, which made

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.400
the insights feel more useful than empty praise.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.480
Researchers are also warning that chatbots can feel so understanding that people share deep

00:05:53.480 --> 00:06:00.860
feelings and start depending on them emotionally. Tech EDT reports. Always on, never judgmental replies

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:06.260
can slowly car out out real human conversations. The way filling a group chat with a bot would

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:12.300
leave less room for actual friends. Treat the tool as a helper, not a stand-in for people you trust,

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:18.200
especially because it remembers past chats and answers at any hour. Meanwhile, the National

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:23.860
Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA, teamed up with IBM on something completely

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.620
different, the Dakota Reports.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:31.540
They released a free AI model trained on nearly two million images from 17 years of

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:32.300
moon photography.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:38.200
The model is called the Lunar Foundation model, meaning a general-purpose learner built first

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.780
on a huge pile of examples, then aimed at science jobs.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.440
It studied pictures from the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter, a spacecraft that photographs the

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.980
moon, and it cut errors in predicting polar ice deposits.

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:56.540
Because the model is open, anyone can download it for a school project or a space hobby,

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.900
which is AI doing real science rather than just chat.

